; Cranelisp — traits and constrained polymorphism
; Traits are shared behaviour. The prelude's operators ARE trait methods.

; /sig shows where an operator comes from — + is the Num trait's method:

/sig +
/sig =
/sig show

; The same + dispatches on the argument type at each call:

(+ 1 2)
(+ 1.0 2.5)

; A function with no type annotation infers its trait bounds. double needs
; Num (it uses +), so it works for every Num type and nothing else:

(defn double [x] (+ x x))
(double 5)
(double 2.5)

; clamp uses < and >, so it infers an Ord bound across all three params:

(defn clamp [x lo hi] (if (< x lo) lo (if (> x hi) hi x)))
(clamp 15 0 10)
(clamp -3 0 10)

; Inference isn't limited to one signature. A defn can carry several clauses —
; a multi-signature function — and each clause is inferred as if the clauses
; were separate mutually-recursive functions. Types flow ACROSS clauses through
; ordinary call resolution: the 1-arg clause calls the 2-arg clause, which pins
; its parameter. No annotation needed anywhere:

(defn grow ([x] (grow x x)) ([a b] (+ (+ a b) 1)))

; A bare name reports every clause's inferred signature:

grow
(grow 5)
(grow 3 4)

; Define your own trait. A method signature names its receiver position:

(deftype Box (MkBox [:Int w :Int h]))
(deftrait Sizeable (size [x] Int))

; impl supplies the method body for one type:

(impl Sizeable Box (defn size [b] (match b [(MkBox w h) (* w h)])))
(size (MkBox 3 4))

; impl confirms what it wired up — and it names the CANONICAL HOME of both the
; trait and the target, not the bare spelling you typed. Extend the same trait
; to a primitive and the confirmation resolves Int to its home, primitives/Int:

(impl Sizeable Int (defn size [n] n))
(size 42)

; The receiver can be a PRELUDE trait, extended to YOUR type. show lives in
; text.display; the confirmation names that home even though you typed a bare
; Display — so you always know exactly which trait and type you wired together:

(impl Display Box (defn show [b] (match b [(MkBox w h) (str (show w) "x" (show h))])))
(show (MkBox 3 4))

; A function constrained on YOUR trait works for any type that implements it:

(defn bigger? [a b] (> (size a) (size b)))
(bigger? (MkBox 5 5) (MkBox 3 4))

; Traits can also be higher-kinded — parameterised by a type CONSTRUCTOR (like
; Option or List), not a plain type. (Functor f) abstracts over any f that holds
; a value; fmap transforms what's inside without touching the shape:

(deftrait (Functor f) (fmap [:(Fn [a] b) func :(f a) x] (f b)))

; A higher-kinded impl ECHOES the declared head (Functor f) in slot 1 and names
; the trait-constructor pairing (Functor Option) in slot 2:

(impl (Functor f) (Functor Option) (defn fmap [func opt] (match opt [None None (Some x) (Some (func x))])))
(fmap (+ 1) (Some 41))
(fmap (* 10) None)

; Most methods are chosen by their arguments. A few are chosen by their RETURN
; type — zed takes no arguments and yields a value of its own type:

(deftrait Zeroable (zed [] self))
(impl Zeroable Int (defn zed [] 0))
(impl Zeroable Float (defn zed [] 0.0))

; A bare name always self-documents — here, zed's polymorphic scheme:

zed

; Call it with nothing to pin the return type and inference has no way to choose
; an impl. The REPL says exactly what to do — an actionable type error, never an
; opaque internal failure:

(zed)

; Annotate the type you want, and dispatch resolves to that impl:

:Int (zed)
:Float (zed)

; This is the machinery behind show, =, <, and + — one name, many types,
; resolved by inference, or pinned by a :Type annotation when the return type
; alone selects the impl. The Sudoku domain uses Eq and Ord the same way.
